Chapter: GconCdxVerdictTest
  cites Works chapter GopConsole
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A8. The desk's `compile` verb ends by comparing what the guest produced
 against the `CODEX.CDX` on the same volume. The launch above it needs VT-x
 and no bed has any, so the comparison is exercised here against a buffer
 poked by hand: every answer the verdict can give is reachable without a
 guest, which is the half of the loop that was waiting on metal for no reason.

 The two length arms are the point rather than decoration. A guest that halts
 early and a guest that emits the wrong bytes both read as DIFFERS, and the
 desk has no serial port to go back and ask (L-SHORT).

  poke-list : Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  poke-list (buf) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf i (list-at xs i)
    in poke-list buf xs (i + 1)

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let buf = alloc-zeroed 16 8
    in let w = poke-list buf [1, 2, 3, 4] 0
    in act
      print-line-uni ("identical      : " & gcon-cdx-verdict [1, 2, 3, 4] buf 4)
      print-line-uni ("byte differs   : " & gcon-cdx-verdict [1, 2, 9, 4] buf 4)
      print-line-uni ("first byte     : " & gcon-cdx-verdict [7, 2, 3, 4] buf 4)
      print-line-uni ("produced short : " & gcon-cdx-verdict [1, 2] buf 4)
      print-line-uni ("produced long  : " & gcon-cdx-verdict [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] buf 4)
      print-line-uni ("produced none  : " & gcon-cdx-verdict [] buf 4)
      print-line-uni ("both empty     : " & gcon-cdx-verdict [] buf 0)
      print-line-uni "done"
    end
